Proposed addition to srfi-279's own 279.sld (adds a `curry` cond-expand
clause alongside the existing chibi/kawa/guile/else clauses). curry
advertises itself as the `curry` feature identifier via `(features)`,
so this clause is selected automatically under curry's own cond-expand.

--- a/srfi/279.sld
+++ b/srfi/279.sld
@@ -46,6 +46,15 @@
        (import (kawa lib reflection))
        (import (kawa lib ports))
        (include "kawa.scm"))
+    (curry
+     (import (srfi 1))   ;; last-pair, every
+     (import (srfi 4))   ;; Typed numeric vectors (u8..s64)
+     (import (srfi 14))  ;; Char sets
+     (import (srfi 69))  ;; Hash tables
+     (import (srfi 111)) ;; Boxes
+     (import (srfi 113)) ;; Sets and bags
+     (import (curry f64vector)) ;; f64vector -- a separate module from srfi 4 in curry
+     (include "curry.scm"))
     (guile
      (import (srfi 1)) ;; List library
      (import (srfi 2)) ;; and-let*

Notes:
- Placed alongside the other named-implementation clauses, before `guile`
  (order among clauses doesn't matter functionally; cond-expand tries
  the current implementation's own feature identifier first regardless
  of position).
- curry.scm needs no `(scheme eval)`/`(scheme complex)`/etc beyond what
  279.sld's own top-level `(import ...)` block already provides — those
  cover `eval`/`interaction-environment` (used for symbol-value) and
  `real-part`/`imag-part` (used for number-properties).
- Verified end-to-end against a live curry build (curry.scm `include`d
  into a define-library with exactly these imports): numbers, pairs,
  strings, vectors, typed numeric vectors (u8vector and the separate
  f64vector module), bytevectors, hash tables, boxes, char-sets,
  records, record-types, and procedures all produce correct
  inspect-properties/inspect-describe output.
